  "articleSummary": "**Summary:**  \nThe article argues that hiring an AI engineer should focus on evaluating the candidate’s judgment and ownership of real‑world AI work, not just on flashy demos or résumé buzzwords. It proposes a concrete hiring process: first write a concrete 90‑day outcome for the role, then give candidates a short, paid “real‑work” task and assess the evidence they produce (e.g., traces, eval reports, design docs) to see how they handle permissions, failures, cost, and monitoring. The piece breaks down AI‑related roles (research, application engineering, infrastructure, evaluation, product engineering) and maps each to the specific artifacts interviewers should look for, and supplies five interview questions that probe measurement, failure handling, and system boundaries. The tone is a practical tutorial aimed at product managers, founders, and engineering leaders who need to hire AI talent without being misled by superficial demos.",
